In most of the code I've been involved with, the migration has
been in the opposite direction.  Code developed under Unix, then
migrated to Windows.  But the problems we've had have often been
just a case of migrating from one version of the compiler to the
next: code which compiled with g++ 2.95.2 doesn't compile with
3.4.x, and code which compiles with 3.4.x doesn't compile with
4.1.0.  And of course, nothing which compiles with g++ compiles
with Sun CC, and vice versa.

> Pre-STL there were more problems, but primarily with the build
> process, or with a non-standard zoo of libraries, less with
> the language itself. But, frankly, in the C++ projects were I
> had been involved (as head or coder) we were very careful; we
> had our test environment on all platforms we delivered for,
> turned compiler warning levels to a high value, made code
> reviews as quality assurance means, and had own standards to
> support writing portable code.

Standard procedure in an industrial environment.  And it does
make C++ quite manageable.  The problem here is that we won't
have access to all of the target environments---we may not even
have heard of some of them.  So we don't know what problems
await us in order to look for them in code reviews.

As I said, if you're targeting a finite, known set of compilers,
no problem.

> >>[...]

> > You'll still be miles away from Java when it comes to
> > portability.

> I am less enthusiastic with that language. Java was missing
> essential language parts for a far too long time.

It still is.  I find it very frustrating to have to mix my
implementation and its specification, for example (the class
definition and the implementation of functions in it).  But it
is usable, with an effort.

> Meanwhile you have them but must take care in what
> environment/version you're working; all that "obsolescent"
> concept, all those many parallel existing GUI libraries;
> nothing that makes me feel comfortable when talking about
> portability.

It's certainly not as portable as they would have you believe.
I've got to do some more experiments shortly.  Class files
generated with 1.6 at work didn't run with 1.5 at home.  If
that's really the case, and they weren't corrupted somewhere in
the transfer, then forget everything I've said about
portability.  But otherwise, there's still a definite advantage
of not having users compiling the code themselves on unknown
platforms.

> > On the other hand, I played around with some Java this
> > afternoon (not having used it in the last five years), and
> > have noticed two things: on platforms other than PC's, it
> > can be awfully slow (the JVM hasn't been optimized), and the
> > code I wrote earlier in the afternoon, and compiled with
> > Java 1.6, doesn't compile with Java 1.5 (so much for
> > portability).

> Language with intermediate interpreted code suffer from
> performance losses; there's not much one can do - besides
> having a good public relations unit.

That's not necessarily true.  In fact, several experts in
optimization that I know claim precisely the contrary---that you
can get far better performance if the final optimization only
occurs in the actual runtime environment.  Sun's JVM for Windows
definitely does a better job of optimizing than CygWin's g++, or
from what little I've seen, VC++.

> Frankly, I am also not convinced that portability and
> interpreted intermediate code are an inseparable prerequisite,
> especially if I have to install a specific runtime environment
> of a certain version.

Supposedly, the JVM specification hasn't changed since it was
made public.  There may be problems with the standard library,
but I doubt that they're that big.

> > I still think the Java portability model is ideal for games
> > and such.  If it really works.

> Life would be so fine if it (life) would really work.

Let's just say that the Java portability model has some features
which could make it preferrable.  Whether actual implementations
screw them up has yet to be determined.  One thing I do know,
however, is that C++ code compiled for a Sparc under Solaris
doesn't work at all under Windows on a PC, and that you often
need significant modifications in the sources to make it work.

